WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.012
Welcome.

2
00:00:01.012 --> 00:00:05.293
This is the narrated briefing for
a companion guide in the Federal

3
00:00:05.293 --> 00:00:10.488
Modernization Workbook: Non-Person
Identity, Zero Trust, SASE and Other Clouds.

4
00:00:10.488 --> 00:00:12.725
The guide's idea fits in one line.

5
00:00:12.725 --> 00:00:15.474
A G M S A fixes the
password, not the protocol:

6
00:00:15.474 --> 00:00:20.816
the service still speaks Kerberos to Active
Directory, where Conditional Access,

7
00:00:20.816 --> 00:00:25.137
Global Secure Access and the Zero
Trust Maturity Model can't see it.

8
00:00:25.137 --> 00:00:28.137
The Non-Person Identity
track has three guides.

9
00:00:28.137 --> 00:00:31.012
The implementation guide
runs the track as gates.

10
00:00:31.012 --> 00:00:34.037
The service accounts guide
moves one account at a time.

11
00:00:34.037 --> 00:00:39.100
This one is the why: what each choice does
to the program's Zero Trust maturity and

12
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:42.705
its SASE design, workload by
workload and cloud by cloud,

13
00:00:42.705 --> 00:00:45.850
and which certificates
each move adds or removes.

14
00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:51.934
Every fact in this deck comes from the
guide, which footnotes each one: CISA,

15
00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:58.313
Microsoft Learn, Red Hat, OMB, AWS and
Oracle, read on November twenty-fourth, 2026.

16
00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:02.321
Where the guide gives the program's
own reading rather than a source's,

17
00:01:02.321 --> 00:01:04.924
it says design guidance,
and so does this deck.

18
00:01:04.924 --> 00:01:08.027
We'll go in four parts: why
a G M S A isn't the end,

19
00:01:08.027 --> 00:01:13.255
what the Zero Trust scorecard and
SASE say, which workloads it touches,

20
00:01:13.255 --> 00:01:18.080
and how certificates, N T L M and
other clouds change the answer.

21
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.755
Here's the answer in
brief, in five points.

22
00:01:24.755 --> 00:01:31.692
First, a G M S A or a D M S A is a better
password, not a Zero Trust identity.

23
00:01:31.692 --> 00:01:35.603
It removes the password a person knows,
which is the Kerberoasting risk,

24
00:01:35.603 --> 00:01:39.979
but the workload still authenticates
with Kerberos against Active Directory.

25
00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:44.350
Nothing in Entra I D evaluates that
sign-in, so Conditional Access,

26
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.830
Entra's risk signals and the
sign-in logs never see it.

27
00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:52.988
Second, a Microsoft Entra workload identity
moves the identity into the program's

28
00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:55.643
central store, with no
secret anyone holds,

29
00:01:55.643 --> 00:02:00.649
a managed identity or a federated credential,
or with a short-lived certificate,

30
00:02:00.649 --> 00:02:03.380
and every token it gets
is logged in Entra I D.

31
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:08.017
The limit, from Microsoft Learn:
Conditional Access for workload identities

32
00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:12.292
covers single-tenant service principals
only, not managed identities.

33
00:02:12.292 --> 00:02:17.343
Third, SASE protects the people in front
of a service, not the service itself.

34
00:02:17.343 --> 00:02:21.851
Private Access and application proxy
put Entra sign-in and phishing-resistant

35
00:02:21.851 --> 00:02:25.540
multifactor authentication in
front of a Kerberos application,

36
00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.092
then reach it with Kerberos
constrained delegation to its S P N.

37
00:02:29.092 --> 00:02:32.936
The application's own service account
and S P N stay where they are,

38
00:02:32.936 --> 00:02:36.067
and service-to-service
traffic doesn't go through the

39
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:39.555
Global Secure Access client,
which runs on end-user devices.

40
00:02:39.555 --> 00:02:42.480
Fourth, certificates
change shape, not count.

41
00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.859
Fifth, extend Entra I D for
people across every cloud,

42
00:02:45.859 --> 00:02:49.080
but don't force one workload
identity across them.

43
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:53.260
That last one is the program's
recommendation, design guidance.

44
00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:02.709
CISA's Zero Trust Maturity Model defines
an identity as attributes that describe,

45
00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:06.885
quote, an agency user or entity,
including non-person entities.

46
00:03:06.885 --> 00:03:10.860
So service accounts are inside the
Identity pillar, not beside it.

47
00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.437
The guide scores three kinds of service
identity against the pillar's functions:

48
00:03:15.437 --> 00:03:19.672
authentication, identity stores, risk
assessments and access management.

49
00:03:19.672 --> 00:03:21.057
The stages are CISA's;

50
00:03:21.057 --> 00:03:25.060
where each kind lands is the
program's reading, design guidance.

51
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:26.298
Authentication.

52
00:03:26.298 --> 00:03:30.750
CISA's Traditional stage is passwords
or multi-factor authentication;

53
00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.422
Optimal continuously validates identity.

54
00:03:33.422 --> 00:03:37.883
A user account used as a service
is Traditional: a static password,

55
00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:42.097
often never expiring, and crackable
offline if it holds an S P N.

56
00:03:42.097 --> 00:03:46.418
A G M S A or a D M S A is better
than Traditional on the credential,

57
00:03:46.418 --> 00:03:50.219
because it's generated,
rotated and unknown to people,

58
00:03:50.219 --> 00:03:54.885
but its Kerberos access is static,
with no continuous evaluation.

59
00:03:54.885 --> 00:03:58.971
An Entra workload identity is
token-based, short-lived and logged,

60
00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:02.073
and a managed identity has
no credential to steal.

61
00:04:02.073 --> 00:04:06.409
Microsoft Learn says workloads can't
perform multifactor authentication,

62
00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:09.300
so the workload equivalent
of phishing-resistant

63
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.535
multifactor authentication
is having no stored secret.

64
00:04:12.535 --> 00:04:13.910
Identity stores.

65
00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:16.791
Traditional is self-managed,
on-premises stores;

66
00:04:16.791 --> 00:04:21.185
Optimal securely integrates them
across all partners and environments.

67
00:04:21.185 --> 00:04:26.685
The user account and the G M S A both live
only in on-premises Active Directory.

68
00:04:26.685 --> 00:04:29.243
The workload identity lives in Entra I D,

69
00:04:29.243 --> 00:04:33.980
the program's integrated store, and
federation reaches other clouds.

70
00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:39.193
Risk assessments.

71
00:04:39.193 --> 00:04:42.530
CISA's Optimal stage
determines risk in real time.

72
00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:47.764
For a user account or a G M S A, the
only signal is Defender for Identity,

73
00:04:47.764 --> 00:04:52.530
on domain controller traffic; a G
M S A gets no Entra risk signal.

74
00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:56.689
A workload identity gets Entra I D
Protection's risk, and Conditional

75
00:04:56.689 --> 00:05:00.993
Access for workload identities, for
single-tenant service principals.

76
00:05:00.993 --> 00:05:02.417
Access management.

77
00:05:02.417 --> 00:05:05.861
Traditional is permanent
access with periodic review;

78
00:05:05.861 --> 00:05:08.705
Optimal is just-in-time
and just-enough access.

79
00:05:08.705 --> 00:05:12.155
A user account has standing
group memberships and delegation.

80
00:05:12.155 --> 00:05:17.392
A G M S A is standing too, scoped by its
retriever group and the account's rights.

81
00:05:17.392 --> 00:05:21.706
A workload identity's role assignments
and app roles are still standing,

82
00:05:21.706 --> 00:05:26.092
but they're visible, scoped per resource,
and reviewable in Entra and Azure.

83
00:05:26.092 --> 00:05:28.655
So what does this mean
for the program's scores?

84
00:05:28.655 --> 00:05:32.844
Every account kept on Kerberos, with
the disposition Keep or Keep Kerberos,

85
00:05:32.844 --> 00:05:36.965
holds the Identity pillar's identity
stores function at Traditional for

86
00:05:36.965 --> 00:05:41.018
that workload until its end date,
whatever the rest of the estate reaches.

87
00:05:41.018 --> 00:05:44.667
That's the program's
decision W D one sixty-five.

88
00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:48.742
The Zero Trust assessment guide, and
the chapter Close Before You Assess,

89
00:05:48.742 --> 00:05:53.429
already count legacy authentication that
service accounts still use as a finding.

90
00:05:53.429 --> 00:05:58.514
The island list, from the Non-Person
Identity developer workbook's chapter

91
00:05:58.514 --> 00:06:03.250
on G M S As, D M S As and the Kerberos
island, is that finding's evidence.

92
00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:12.350
The program's SASE is Microsoft's security
service edge, Global Secure Access.

93
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.650
Three of its parts touch service accounts.

94
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:19.788
First, Private Access and application
proxy, with Kerberos single sign-on.

95
00:06:19.788 --> 00:06:23.638
The user signs in to Entra I
D, under Conditional Access.

96
00:06:23.638 --> 00:06:27.231
Then, in Microsoft Learn's words,
the connector impersonates

97
00:06:27.231 --> 00:06:30.250
the user to get a Kerberos
token to the application.

98
00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:34.120
That needs the connector and the
application server domain joined,

99
00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:37.575
and the application's S P N in
the connector's delegation list.

100
00:06:37.575 --> 00:06:41.900
The application keeps running as its
service account, which holds the S P N;

101
00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:46.225
Learn's example is a web service account
that's a user or a computer account.

102
00:06:46.225 --> 00:06:50.926
So moving that S P N to a G M S A, at
step A5 of the service accounts guide,

103
00:06:50.926 --> 00:06:55.713
means updating the connector's
delegation entry in the same change.

104
00:06:55.713 --> 00:06:58.726
Second, Private Access
for domain controllers.

105
00:06:58.726 --> 00:07:03.326
It puts Conditional Access and
phishing-resistant multifactor authentication in

106
00:07:03.326 --> 00:07:07.006
front of Kerberos to on-premises
applications, for the users who are

107
00:07:07.006 --> 00:07:10.950
synchronized from Active Directory,
and its sensor starts in audit mode.

108
00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.438
It's scoped to users.

109
00:07:12.438 --> 00:07:17.300
Once it's enforced, Kerberos to a protected
S P N from a device without the Global

110
00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:19.320
Secure Access client is blocked,

111
00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.687
so service-to-service Kerberos
needs an S P N exclusion.

112
00:07:22.687 --> 00:07:25.607
Third, the Global Secure
Access client controls

113
00:07:25.607 --> 00:07:28.599
network traffic at the
end-user computing device.

114
00:07:28.599 --> 00:07:32.224
A server-side workload's own
traffic isn't in the SASE path;

115
00:07:32.224 --> 00:07:36.075
its protection is its identity
and its network segmentation.

116
00:07:36.075 --> 00:07:39.125
So SASE and this track meet at the S P N.

117
00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:43.422
A Kerberos application published
through Private Access is Zero Trust at

118
00:07:43.422 --> 00:07:47.950
the front door, and Traditional behind
it, until its service account moves.

119
00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:53.407
Now the workloads, one by one,

120
00:07:53.407 --> 00:07:57.513
each with its target from the service
accounts guide's decision path.

121
00:07:57.513 --> 00:08:02.829
The SQL Server engine runs as
a user account or a G M S A,

122
00:08:02.829 --> 00:08:04.812
with an M S S Q L S V C S P N.

123
00:08:04.812 --> 00:08:07.660
Its G M S A stays the
Windows logon account,

124
00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:12.350
and the instance gets an Entra
identity: the Arc managed identity on

125
00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:17.124
SQL Server 2025, or an app registration
with a certificate on 2022.

126
00:08:17.124 --> 00:08:20.122
Users and applications then
sign in with Entra logins;

127
00:08:20.122 --> 00:08:23.586
Microsoft Learn lists
non-interactive mechanisms including

128
00:08:23.586 --> 00:08:27.050
managed identities for SQL
Server's Entra authentication.

129
00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:29.189
Users reach it through Private Access,

130
00:08:29.189 --> 00:08:33.400
but the engine's Kerberos stays on the
island until its logins are Entra-only.

131
00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:36.549
Applications that call SQL Server run as a

132
00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:39.450
user account or a G M S
A with a Windows login.

133
00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:43.935
Their target is a managed identity, or
a service principal with a certificate,

134
00:08:43.935 --> 00:08:46.038
with an Entra login in SQL Server,

135
00:08:46.038 --> 00:08:49.262
so the connection moves from
Kerberos to an Entra token.

136
00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:52.462
That's server to server, so
it's not in the SASE path.

137
00:08:52.462 --> 00:08:56.187
I I S sites and application pools run as a

138
00:08:56.187 --> 00:08:59.687
user account or a G M S
A with an H T T P S P N.

139
00:08:59.687 --> 00:09:04.987
Their target is a G M S A now, and a managed
identity when the site moves to Azure.

140
00:09:04.987 --> 00:09:10.053
Users sign in with Entra through
application proxy or Private Access,

141
00:09:10.053 --> 00:09:13.197
with Kerberos single sign-on
to the H T T P S P N,

142
00:09:13.197 --> 00:09:18.040
and the pool's own calls to Microsoft
A P Is use an Entra identity.

143
00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.851
Scheduled tasks and Windows
services often run as

144
00:09:24.851 --> 00:09:27.790
a user account with a
password that never expires.

145
00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:30.957
Their target is a G M S A
now, and an Azure Automation

146
00:09:30.957 --> 00:09:34.253
runbook with a managed
identity once the job moves.

147
00:09:34.253 --> 00:09:38.577
The password goes; the identity moves
to Entra only when the job does.

148
00:09:38.577 --> 00:09:40.490
They're not in the SASE path.

149
00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:46.314
Red Hat Enterprise Linux and other Linux
services run on an Active Directory-joined

150
00:09:46.314 --> 00:09:48.893
host, with S S S D and
realm D and a keytab,

151
00:09:48.893 --> 00:09:53.802
or with Red Hat's own Managed Service
Account, made with A D C L I create M S A.

152
00:09:53.802 --> 00:09:58.526
Their target is the platform's workload
identity: an I A M role on E C 2,

153
00:09:58.526 --> 00:10:03.852
an instance principal on O C I, or the
Arc managed identity for calls to Azure.

154
00:10:03.852 --> 00:10:07.067
A keytab on disk is a
long-lived Kerberos key,

155
00:10:07.067 --> 00:10:11.889
unlike a Windows G M S A; the platform
identity has no stored secret.

156
00:10:11.889 --> 00:10:15.810
Workloads in AWS GovCloud
U S run as I A M roles,

157
00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.565
or as I A M users with access keys.

158
00:10:18.565 --> 00:10:24.082
Their target is an I A M role, and to call
Microsoft A P Is, a federated credential,

159
00:10:24.082 --> 00:10:28.565
once it's tested; that's the program's
decision W D one seventy-one.

160
00:10:28.565 --> 00:10:32.090
They get temporary credentials,
with keys as exceptions,

161
00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:36.515
and people reach AWS through I A M
Identity Center federated with Entra.

162
00:10:36.515 --> 00:10:38.669
O C I workloads use instance,

163
00:10:38.669 --> 00:10:42.978
resource or workload principals,
or users with A P I keys.

164
00:10:42.978 --> 00:10:47.566
The target is a principal, with keys as
exceptions, so there's no stored secret,

165
00:10:47.566 --> 00:10:52.180
and people reach O C I through its
identity domain federated with Entra.

166
00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:58.005
Linux deserves a closer look.

167
00:10:58.005 --> 00:11:00.829
Red Hat recommends joining a R H E L host

168
00:11:00.829 --> 00:11:03.793
to Active Directory
with S S S D and realm.

169
00:11:03.793 --> 00:11:09.197
Its Managed Service Account is an Active
Directory account that corresponds to a

170
00:11:09.197 --> 00:11:12.456
specific computer, created
with A D C L I create M S A,

171
00:11:12.456 --> 00:11:15.954
and its Kerberos key is written
to a keytab on the host.

172
00:11:15.954 --> 00:11:18.643
That's a different thing
from a Windows G M S A.

173
00:11:18.643 --> 00:11:23.192
The key sits in a file, so it's a
long-lived secret to protect and rotate.

174
00:11:23.192 --> 00:11:28.298
For people, Microsoft Learn says Entra I
D can be the sign-in for S S H on Azure

175
00:11:28.298 --> 00:11:32.651
Linux virtual machines and on
Azure Arc-enabled Linux servers,

176
00:11:32.651 --> 00:11:37.171
and it lists R H E L seven point four
to seven point nine, eight point

177
00:11:37.171 --> 00:11:41.942
three and later, nine point oh and
later, and ten point one as supported.

178
00:11:41.942 --> 00:11:46.798
An R H E L instance in AWS GovCloud
U S or O C I that's onboarded to Arc

179
00:11:46.798 --> 00:11:52.479
can use the same Entra sign-in, so
operators keep one identity across clouds.

180
00:11:52.479 --> 00:11:55.096
That's design guidance,
and the Arc track guide

181
00:11:55.096 --> 00:11:58.100
covers onboarding other
clouds' servers to Arc.

182
00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:06.850
Moving a service account or its S P N to
Entra changes which certificates exist.

183
00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:09.913
Each row starts from what
the workload holds today,

184
00:12:09.913 --> 00:12:14.287
and an S P N needs no certificate, as
the service accounts guide explains.

185
00:12:14.287 --> 00:12:15.988
Start with SQL Server.

186
00:12:15.988 --> 00:12:20.666
An engine running as a user account
or a G M S A, with Windows logins,

187
00:12:20.666 --> 00:12:24.550
has an optional T L S
certificate; Kerberos needs none.

188
00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:28.994
Add Entra logins, and every instance that
takes them needs a T L S certificate

189
00:12:28.994 --> 00:12:33.650
issued by a certificate authority, with
the service account able to read its key.

190
00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:36.671
CyberArk Certificate Manager
issues and renews it,

191
00:12:36.671 --> 00:12:40.113
from the program's Active
Directory Certificate Services.

192
00:12:40.113 --> 00:12:45.423
A SQL Server 2022 instance's Entra identity
adds an app-registration certificate in

193
00:12:45.423 --> 00:12:50.225
Key Vault, which Key Vault and the Arc
extension handle when it's service-managed.

194
00:12:50.225 --> 00:12:54.488
A SQL Server 2025 instance
uses the Arc managed identity:

195
00:12:54.488 --> 00:12:58.325
no certificate before, none
after, and Azure handles it.

196
00:12:58.325 --> 00:13:01.882
An I I S pool published through
application proxy or Private

197
00:13:01.882 --> 00:13:05.937
Access with Kerberos single sign-on
keeps the same site certificate.

198
00:13:05.937 --> 00:13:10.425
The connector reaches the S P N with
Kerberos, so there's no new certificate.

199
00:13:10.425 --> 00:13:14.093
CyberArk Certificate Manager
handles the site's certificate,

200
00:13:14.093 --> 00:13:17.650
and Microsoft handles the
application proxy endpoint.

201
00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:23.037
Now the rest of the map.

202
00:13:23.037 --> 00:13:26.748
An application that calls
Microsoft Graph as a user account,

203
00:13:26.748 --> 00:13:31.036
moved to an app registration, gains
an app-registration certificate,

204
00:13:31.036 --> 00:13:35.249
at most a hundred and eighty days,
renewed by its owner or by Key Vault.

205
00:13:35.249 --> 00:13:39.509
The same application on Azure or on
an Arc-enabled server moves to a

206
00:13:39.509 --> 00:13:44.000
managed identity instead: none before,
none after, and Azure handles it.

207
00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:49.081
A workload outside AWS that uses an I A M
user's secret key can move to I A M Roles

208
00:13:49.081 --> 00:13:52.710
Anywhere, which needs an X
dot five-oh-nine certificate

209
00:13:52.710 --> 00:13:55.775
from the program's public
key infrastructure.

210
00:13:55.775 --> 00:13:57.753
The certificate platform issues it,

211
00:13:57.753 --> 00:14:01.050
and GovCloud needs certificate
revocation lists imported.

212
00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:04.233
And a R H E L host's
Active Directory keytab,

213
00:14:04.233 --> 00:14:09.382
moved to the platform's workload
identity, needs no certificate at

214
00:14:09.382 --> 00:14:13.875
all for an I A M role or an O C I
principal; AWS or O C I handles it.

215
00:14:13.875 --> 00:14:16.487
So certificates change shape, not count.

216
00:14:16.487 --> 00:14:20.541
A managed identity, an I A M role
and an O C I principal need none;

217
00:14:20.541 --> 00:14:25.150
an app registration and Roles
Anywhere each need a certificate.

218
00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:30.337
N T L M.

219
00:14:30.337 --> 00:14:35.426
The program's backstop for N
T L M is April first, 2027.

220
00:14:35.426 --> 00:14:38.915
It's the program's own date,
not an external deadline;

221
00:14:38.915 --> 00:14:41.075
that's decision W D twenty-seven.

222
00:14:41.075 --> 00:14:43.200
It applies to service accounts too.

223
00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:48.537
After that date, an account kept in Active
Directory, as Keep or Keep Kerberos,

224
00:14:48.537 --> 00:14:51.756
authenticates with Kerberos
only, never N T L M,

225
00:14:51.756 --> 00:14:55.737
and its end date still follows
decision W D one sixty-five.

226
00:14:55.737 --> 00:15:00.624
Microsoft's own position, on Microsoft
Learn, is that N T L M is still supported,

227
00:15:00.624 --> 00:15:04.425
and that Kerberos is the
preferred authentication method.

228
00:15:04.425 --> 00:15:06.375
And watch for the quiet failure.

229
00:15:06.375 --> 00:15:09.160
An S P N that's missing,
or on the wrong account,

230
00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:14.030
is the usual way a service falls back
to N T L M without anyone choosing it.

231
00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:21.555
Should agencies extend Microsoft
Entra I D across clouds?

232
00:15:21.555 --> 00:15:23.330
For people, yes.

233
00:15:23.330 --> 00:15:28.273
O M B memorandum M twenty-two oh-nine says
agencies must employ centralized identity

234
00:15:28.273 --> 00:15:31.098
management systems for
agency users that can be

235
00:15:31.098 --> 00:15:34.205
integrated into applications
and common platforms.

236
00:15:34.205 --> 00:15:37.412
And CISA's Optimal identity
store securely integrates

237
00:15:37.412 --> 00:15:40.005
stores across all partners
and environments.

238
00:15:40.005 --> 00:15:44.680
Entra I D can be that one store for the
program's people in every cloud it uses.

239
00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:51.230
In AWS GovCloud U S, I A M Identity Center
connects your existing identity provider,

240
00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:56.143
and it runs in GovCloud with FIPS
endpoints and A W S U S Gov A R Ns.

241
00:15:56.143 --> 00:15:59.122
The program's chapter on
privileged roles and tiers

242
00:15:59.122 --> 00:16:02.031
already uses its permission
sets for human access.

243
00:16:02.031 --> 00:16:07.517
In O C I, Oracle documents single
sign-on and identity lifecycle management

244
00:16:07.517 --> 00:16:13.092
between O C I I A M and Microsoft Entra
I D, for tenancies with identity domains.

245
00:16:13.092 --> 00:16:17.942
The program's O C two tenancy still
has to confirm it has identity domains.

246
00:16:17.942 --> 00:16:21.348
And on Linux hosts, Entra
sign-in for S S H on

247
00:16:21.348 --> 00:16:25.160
Azure and Arc-enabled
Linux, R H E L included.

248
00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:33.348
For workloads, the answer is no
single store; one record instead.

249
00:16:33.348 --> 00:16:36.586
M twenty-two oh-nine's sentence
is about agency users.

250
00:16:36.586 --> 00:16:40.484
A workload is safest with
its own platform's identity,

251
00:16:40.484 --> 00:16:44.738
which has no stored secret and
never leaves that platform:

252
00:16:44.738 --> 00:16:49.347
an I A M role in AWS, a principal in
O C I, a managed identity in Azure.

253
00:16:49.347 --> 00:16:53.678
Forcing an Entra identity onto an
AWS workload would mean a secret

254
00:16:53.678 --> 00:16:58.323
or a certificate where AWS would
issue temporary credentials for free.

255
00:16:58.323 --> 00:17:00.714
What the program extends across clouds for

256
00:17:00.714 --> 00:17:03.372
workloads is the governance,
not the credential.

257
00:17:03.372 --> 00:17:07.735
One inventory, joined to a system
record with two owners and a sponsor.

258
00:17:07.735 --> 00:17:10.135
One exception rule for long-lived keys.

259
00:17:10.135 --> 00:17:14.546
And federation between platforms
where a workload must call across,

260
00:17:14.546 --> 00:17:19.499
such as an AWS workload calling Microsoft
Graph with a federated credential,

261
00:17:19.499 --> 00:17:22.672
once it's tested; that's
decision W D one seventy-one.

262
00:17:22.672 --> 00:17:26.690
This split is the program's
recommendation, design guidance.

263
00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:32.528
The guide lists five dangers.

264
00:17:32.528 --> 00:17:37.302
Counting a G M S A as done for Zero Trust
leaves the Identity pillar at Traditional

265
00:17:37.302 --> 00:17:41.407
for that workload, unseen: score
kept accounts against the scorecard,

266
00:17:41.407 --> 00:17:44.215
and let the island list
carry them to an end date.

267
00:17:44.215 --> 00:17:49.417
Publishing a Kerberos application through
Private Access and forgetting its service

268
00:17:49.417 --> 00:17:52.196
account leaves the back
end outside Zero Trust,

269
00:17:52.196 --> 00:17:54.333
and an S P N move breaks single sign-on:

270
00:17:54.333 --> 00:17:58.609
record the application's service account
and S P N with the published app,

271
00:17:58.609 --> 00:18:01.815
and move the connector's
delegation in step A5's change.

272
00:18:01.815 --> 00:18:06.491
Enforcing the domain controller sensor
on an S P N a service uses blocks

273
00:18:06.491 --> 00:18:09.344
service-to-service
Kerberos: start in audit,

274
00:18:09.344 --> 00:18:13.227
add S P N exclusions to the
exception register, then enforce.

275
00:18:13.227 --> 00:18:18.750
Treating a R H E L keytab like a G M S A
leaves a long-lived key on disk unrotated:

276
00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:24.452
inventory keytabs as secrets, and move
the workload to its platform identity.

277
00:18:24.452 --> 00:18:29.045
And one Entra identity for every cloud's
workloads puts secrets or certificates

278
00:18:29.045 --> 00:18:33.364
where the platform would issue temporary
credentials: use native workload

279
00:18:33.364 --> 00:18:37.914
identities, one governance record, and
federation only to cross platforms.

280
00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:43.998
To close, the guide's checklist.

281
00:18:43.998 --> 00:18:47.826
Every account kept on Kerberos is
scored against the Identity pillar,

282
00:18:47.826 --> 00:18:49.297
with its end date and owner.

283
00:18:49.297 --> 00:18:54.439
Every Kerberos application published
through Private Access or application proxy

284
00:18:54.439 --> 00:18:56.467
names its service account and S P N,

285
00:18:56.467 --> 00:18:59.798
and the connector's delegation
entry moves with any S P N.

286
00:18:59.798 --> 00:19:03.115
S P N exclusions for
service-to-service Kerberos are in the

287
00:19:03.115 --> 00:19:07.123
exception register before the domain
controller sensor is enforced.

288
00:19:07.123 --> 00:19:11.510
Every SQL Server instance that takes
Entra logins has a T L S certificate

289
00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:15.272
from a certificate authority,
which the service account can read.

290
00:19:15.272 --> 00:19:20.385
No service account uses N T
L M after April first, 2027.

291
00:19:20.385 --> 00:19:25.216
And the program's people sign in to AWS,
O C I and Linux hosts with Entra I D,

292
00:19:25.216 --> 00:19:30.047
while workloads use their platform's
identity, each joined to a record.

293
00:19:30.047 --> 00:19:32.719
For the moves themselves,
go back to the track:

294
00:19:32.719 --> 00:19:37.410
the Non-Person Identity implementation
guide, and the service accounts guide.

295
00:19:37.410 --> 00:19:38.300
Thank you.
